.TH IAXPHONE 1 "November 2026" "iaxphone 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
iaxphone \- IAX2 softphone for the command line
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B iaxphone
[\fIoptions\fR]
.B register
.br
.B iaxphone
[\fIoptions\fR]
.B call
.I number
.br
.B iaxphone
[\fIoptions\fR]
.B ping
.br
.B iaxphone devices
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B iaxphone
speaks IAX2 (RFC 5456), the Inter-Asterisk eXchange protocol, to an
Asterisk server or any other IAX2 peer. It places and takes calls in G.711
u-law or A-law, sends and receives DTMF, holds and transfers calls, rings locally and plays the audio
on the system's sound devices.
.PP
.B register
keeps a registration with the server and takes the calls it sends, answering
each after the
.B \-auto\-answer
delay, until interrupted.
.B call
dials
.I number
and returns when the call ends.
.B ping
measures the round trip to the server.
.B devices
lists the sound devices, marking the defaults with a star; their names are
what
.B \-mic
and
.B \-speaker
take.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-server " host[:port]"
IAX2 server (default 127.0.0.1, port 4569).
.TP
.BI \-user " name"
.TQ
.BI \-secret " secret"
Our account on the server.
.TP
.BI \-callerid " number"
.TQ
.BI \-name " name"
Caller id number and name sent on outgoing calls (default name: iaxphone).
.TP
.BI \-duration " time"
Hang up this long after the answer, for example 30s (default: when the peer
hangs up).
.TP
.BI \-auto\-answer " time"
Ring this long before answering an incoming call (default 2s).
.TP
.BI \-audio " default|none"
Use the system's sound devices, or send silence and discard the audio
(default: default).
.TP
.BI \-mic " name"
.TQ
.BI \-speaker " name"
Capture and playback devices by name, as listed by
.BR devices ;
empty for the system defaults.
.TP
.BI \-jitter " ms"
Jitter buffer depth, 20 to 500 ms (default 60).
.TP
.BI \-codec " list"
Codecs to accept, best first, comma separated: opus, ulaw, alaw (default
all three, opus first, when built with libopus; ulaw,alaw otherwise). The
first one is asked for; the server has the last word.
.TP
.BI \-dtmf " keys"
Keys to press one second after the call is answered, from 0-9, *, # and
A-D.
.TP
.BI \-hold " time"
How long each
.B \-dtmf
key is held (default 100ms).
.TP
.BI \-onhold " time"
Put the call on hold for this long once it is answered, after the
.B \-dtmf
keys: the server stops sending audio and plays music to whoever is at
the other end.
.TP
.BI \-transfer " number"
Transfer the call to this number once it is answered, after
.B \-dtmf
and
.BR \-onhold :
whoever is at the other end is sent there (a blind transfer) and the
server hangs up on us. A context may follow the number after an @.
.TP
.B \-v
Log every full frame and the audio levels once a second.
.SH EXAMPLES
Register with the server and take calls:
.PP
.nf
.RS
iaxphone \-server pbx \-user phone \-secret s3cret register
.RE
.fi
.PP
Call extension 603, hold the keys 1234 for 300 ms each once answered:
.PP
.nf
.RS
iaxphone \-server pbx \-user phone \-secret s3cret \-dtmf 1234 \-hold 300ms call 603
.RE
.fi
.PP
Call 2001, hold the call for 5 s, then transfer it to 2002:
.PP
.nf
.RS
iaxphone \-server pbx \-user phone \-secret s3cret \-onhold 5s \-transfer 2002 call 2001
.RE
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR iaxphone\-gui (1)
